1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,800
Usually, when we sit down to read a mystery novel, there's this, you know, this built-in expectation of a journey.

2
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:07,660
Right, yeah. You expect a narrative.

3
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:14,600
Exactly. Like, you read chapter one, the crime happens. Chapter two, the detective shows up and, I don't know, finds a muddy footprint or something.

4
00:00:14,820 --> 00:00:16,680
Yeah, then chapter three, they interview the suspect.

5
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:23,840
Right. You follow the sequence of events as they unfold over time. The story literally is the sequence.

6
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:27,520
Because the history of what happened is the entire point.

7
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:31,580
I mean, without that history, the narrative just, it doesn't exist.

8
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:39,000
But imagine if instead of handing you a 300-page book, the author just handed you, like, a single index card.

9
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:43,460
And on that index card, it just said, the butler is currently in jail.

10
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:45,360
That would be a terrible book.

11
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:48,240
Right. You'd be holding the state, but you would have completely lost the history.

12
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:50,980
Yeah, you'd look at it and say, well, okay, but what happened?

13
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:55,380
Exactly. Who was murdered? Why did the butler do it? Did he act alone?

14
00:00:55,380 --> 00:01:01,680
You have the final configuration of the characters, you know, but the journey is just completely gone.

15
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:02,440
It's all erased.

16
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:06,120
And here is the wild part for you listening today.

17
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:08,080
That exact scenario.

18
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:08,820
The index card.

19
00:01:08,980 --> 00:01:12,020
Yeah. Throwing away the whole book and just keeping the final index card.

20
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:19,140
That is the fundamental mechanism behind almost every piece of software running our modern world.

21
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:20,200
It really is.

22
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:25,140
It's how we've been taught to program computers for, I mean, decades now.

23
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:28,940
We are just handing the machine index cards and immediately throwing away the book.

24
00:01:29,020 --> 00:01:32,600
And then, you know, we act surprised when the machine does something completely unexpected.

25
00:01:32,940 --> 00:01:33,180
Right.

26
00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:34,920
And we have absolutely no idea why.

27
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:40,900
We've built an entire digital infrastructure on this premise that the current moment is the only thing that matters.

28
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:43,280
Which is just a massive philosophical assumption.

29
00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:43,900
Huge.

30
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:45,340
And a dangerous one.

31
00:01:45,340 --> 00:01:48,520
Which brings us to the core of today's deep dive.

32
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:52,800
We are unpacking some truly mind-bending source material for you today.

33
00:01:52,840 --> 00:01:53,780
Yeah, this one is dense.

34
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:54,320
It is.

35
00:01:54,400 --> 00:02:01,800
We have our hands on a highly philosophical 2026 monograph from Flixion Independent Research.

36
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:02,120
Right.

37
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:07,700
It is titled Programs as Histories, Projection, Admissibility, and the Geometry of Computation.

38
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:08,540
Quite a mouthful.

39
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:14,360
Yeah, but we're exploring a conceptual programming language detailed in this text, and it's called SpherePop.

40
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:15,160
SpherePop.

41
00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:19,440
And SpherePop claims everything we know about computing is entirely backward.

42
00:02:19,960 --> 00:02:26,120
So the goal today is to kind of, you know, cut through the dense math in this paper and get to this paradigm-shifting realization.

43
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:27,240
The big aha moment.

44
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:27,820
Exactly.

45
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:32,180
The realization that computation is not the manipulation of values.

46
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:35,440
It is the progressive restriction of possibility.

47
00:02:35,740 --> 00:02:37,840
Which is just a beautiful way to phrase it.

48
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:44,500
And, you know, the backdrop visual we have up right now, this abstract geometry shifting and crystallizing into cascading lines of code.

49
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:44,820
Yeah.

50
00:02:44,980 --> 00:02:47,840
It really captures what the authors are trying to do here.

51
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:53,700
They are translating highly abstract philosophical geometry into hard, executable reality.

52
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:54,080
Right.

53
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:57,560
They aren't just proposing a new syntax, like a new way to type things.

54
00:02:57,640 --> 00:02:58,320
No, not at all.

55
00:02:58,420 --> 00:03:01,520
They are proposing a whole new ontology of what computing actually is.

56
00:03:01,620 --> 00:03:01,820
Okay.

57
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:02,980
So let's start with the problem.

58
00:03:02,980 --> 00:03:12,420
Because to understand why SpherePop is so revolutionary, we first have to understand the fundamental lie that almost every other programming language is built on.

59
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:15,340
What the text calls the state-centric assumption.

60
00:03:15,820 --> 00:03:16,100
Right.

61
00:03:16,220 --> 00:03:19,500
I mean, when I learned to code, the very first lesson was about variables.

62
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:20,000
Sure.

63
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:20,340
Yeah.

64
00:03:20,340 --> 00:03:27,320
I was taught that a variable is like a little box in the computer's memory, and you put a value in it, like X equals 5.

65
00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:30,340
And then the next instruction might be X equals X plus 1.

66
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:30,600
Right.

67
00:03:30,860 --> 00:03:34,840
So you take the 5 out, add 1, and put the 6 back into the box.

68
00:03:35,240 --> 00:03:35,460
Yeah.

69
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:38,180
So the program starts in an initial state.

70
00:03:38,540 --> 00:03:40,880
The instructions modify that state step by step.

71
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:44,280
And whatever is left in the boxes at the end, well, that's your final answer.

72
00:03:44,580 --> 00:03:46,260
Which feels completely natural, right?

73
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:49,920
Because it mirrors how we naively view physical processes.

74
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:50,460
How so?

75
00:03:50,780 --> 00:03:52,500
Well, an object has properties.

76
00:03:53,020 --> 00:03:54,560
Those properties change over time.

77
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:57,720
And the current properties make up the object's current state.

78
00:03:58,020 --> 00:04:01,660
But the monograph argues this naturalness is actually just an illusion.

79
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:02,920
Yes.

80
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:06,540
The source refers to this explicitly as the state illusion.

81
00:04:06,740 --> 00:04:07,440
The state illusion.

82
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:07,700
Yeah.

83
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:13,680
They argue it's a prior choice by language designers to treat the present configuration as the fundamental unit of description.

84
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:14,280
Right.

85
00:04:14,360 --> 00:04:17,940
Because when you put the 6 into the box, the 5 is just overwritten.

86
00:04:18,140 --> 00:04:18,620
It's gone.

87
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:23,840
The history of that box ever holding a 5 is utterly annihilated from the universe.

88
00:04:24,440 --> 00:04:34,240
And the source uses some fantastic real-world examples to illustrate why treating that final state as the base reality is just the wrong approach.

89
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:34,800
Oh, yeah.

90
00:04:34,980 --> 00:04:36,020
The Git example is great.

91
00:04:36,020 --> 00:04:37,100
I love the Git example.

92
00:04:37,100 --> 00:04:41,140
Think about version control, which developers use literally every single day.

93
00:04:41,460 --> 00:04:46,460
If Git operated under the state illusion, it would just save the current version of your code files.

94
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:47,480
Just the final text.

95
00:04:47,620 --> 00:04:47,840
Right.

96
00:04:47,980 --> 00:04:48,900
But it doesn't do that.

97
00:04:49,160 --> 00:04:53,200
A Git repository's actual substance is the entire sequence of commits.

98
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:55,500
The diffs, the additions, the deletions.

99
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:55,980
Exactly.

100
00:04:56,200 --> 00:04:58,660
All of that history that produced the final file tree.

101
00:04:58,740 --> 00:05:01,520
The final files are just a projection of that history.

102
00:05:01,740 --> 00:05:06,160
Or consider event sourcing and enterprise database architecture.

103
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:06,860
Okay, yeah.

104
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:12,740
If you look at a modern banking system, the database doesn't just store a single cell that says your balance is $500.

105
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:16,820
Because if it did and a glitch randomly changed it to $0.

106
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:19,460
The bank would have absolutely no idea how to fix it.

107
00:05:19,640 --> 00:05:21,020
They'd just be like, well, I guess you're broke.

108
00:05:21,100 --> 00:05:21,680
Right, exactly.

109
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:27,180
So instead, event sourcing means the database records an append-only log of events.

110
00:05:27,320 --> 00:05:30,320
Like a $200 deposit, then a $100 withdrawal.

111
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:32,300
Then a $400 deposit.

112
00:05:32,460 --> 00:05:37,520
And the current state of your balance, that $500, is just a summary calculation.

113
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:38,400
A fold.

114
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:40,740
Yeah, a fold over that entire historical log.

115
00:05:40,740 --> 00:05:42,340
So the state is just a summary.

116
00:05:42,460 --> 00:05:44,160
The history is the actual substance.

117
00:05:44,780 --> 00:05:51,560
But current programming languages like C, Python, Java, they treat the summary as the base reality.

118
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:52,180
They do.

119
00:05:52,320 --> 00:05:56,100
And the monograph goes really deep into the mathematical consequences of this flaw.

120
00:05:56,180 --> 00:05:56,480
Right.

121
00:05:56,680 --> 00:06:02,040
It introduces Proposition 1.2, which is the formal mathematical proof of the state illusion.

122
00:06:02,040 --> 00:06:08,820
Okay, so the paper throws around Proposition 1.2 and mentions non-injective projections.

123
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:09,360
Right.

124
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:14,240
Which honestly just sounds like a lot of really dense math to say, we lose data.

125
00:06:14,500 --> 00:06:17,540
I mean, is that really all it's saying, or is there something deeper there?

126
00:06:17,820 --> 00:06:18,660
Oh, it's definitely deeper.

127
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:22,460
Because it proves that losing data isn't just a minor side effect.

128
00:06:22,620 --> 00:06:24,420
It is a structural inevitability.

129
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:25,820
Okay, break that down for me.

130
00:06:26,040 --> 00:06:27,640
Let's use the Pigeonel principle behind it.

131
00:06:28,080 --> 00:06:31,800
Imagine the set of all possible finite histories of a program.

132
00:06:32,460 --> 00:06:35,780
So every single path a program could ever take.

133
00:06:35,920 --> 00:06:36,300
Exactly.

134
00:06:36,620 --> 00:06:40,640
Every loop it could run, every single piece of user input it could receive over time.

135
00:06:41,040 --> 00:06:43,580
Let's call that infinite space of possibilities H.

136
00:06:43,740 --> 00:06:44,880
Okay, H is infinite.

137
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:45,360
Got it.

138
00:06:45,460 --> 00:06:52,960
Now imagine the space of all possible observable states the computer can actually hold in its memory at any one given moment.

139
00:06:53,020 --> 00:06:53,740
Let's call that S.

140
00:06:53,740 --> 00:06:59,780
And because a computer has finite RAM, you know, physical memory limits, S has to be a finite space.

141
00:06:59,980 --> 00:07:00,440
Precisely.

142
00:07:00,440 --> 00:07:03,620
Even if S is massively huge, it is still finite.

143
00:07:03,860 --> 00:07:04,120
Right.

144
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:13,420
So a program that relies on state is essentially running a function that maps the infinite space of histories, H, into the finite space of states, S.

145
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:14,760
Oh, I see where this is going.

146
00:07:14,860 --> 00:07:17,940
Yeah, because H is infinitely larger than S, you have a mathematical bottleneck.

147
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:18,180
Yeah.

148
00:07:18,180 --> 00:07:20,940
This projection literally cannot be injective.

149
00:07:21,320 --> 00:07:29,520
Meaning it's a mathematical certainty that multiple completely different histories will inevitably collapse into the exact same state.

150
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:32,160
Because you have way more pigeons than you have pigeonholes.

151
00:07:32,360 --> 00:07:32,660
Right.

152
00:07:32,660 --> 00:07:34,320
Some pigeons are going to have to share a whole.

153
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:34,660
Yes.

154
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:43,560
Any program that only exposes that final state projection silently discards distinguishing information.

155
00:07:43,760 --> 00:07:46,500
You can just no longer tell which history actually occurred.

156
00:07:46,640 --> 00:07:46,880
Okay.

157
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:50,160
I want to use an analogy here to make sure we're fully grasping the weight of this.

158
00:07:50,220 --> 00:07:53,760
Because a listener actually brought this up in some feedback we were looking at, and I think it's brilliant.

159
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:54,620
Oh, I'd love to hear it.

160
00:07:54,620 --> 00:07:58,280
So imagine you come home, and your living room is completely destroyed.

161
00:07:58,540 --> 00:07:58,780
Okay.

162
00:07:58,920 --> 00:07:59,400
Bad day.

163
00:07:59,560 --> 00:07:59,780
Right.

164
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:01,680
The lamp is broken on the floor.

165
00:08:02,180 --> 00:08:12,160
Now, in a state-based programming language, the state is simply recorded as lamp location equals floor, lamp status equals broken.

166
00:08:12,440 --> 00:08:15,580
Which tells you absolutely nothing about the causality.

167
00:08:15,700 --> 00:08:16,140
Exactly.

168
00:08:16,140 --> 00:08:21,020
If you're trying to debug this living room, you just have a single photograph of the mess.

169
00:08:21,020 --> 00:08:24,640
You are left staring at this photograph trying to deduce what happened.

170
00:08:24,780 --> 00:08:26,180
I mean, did the cat knock it over?

171
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:26,840
Was there an earthquake?

172
00:08:27,380 --> 00:08:28,600
Did a burglar break in?

173
00:08:28,740 --> 00:08:29,840
You're just guessing at that point.

174
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:34,820
But if you were using a history-based system, you wouldn't just see the final state.

175
00:08:35,040 --> 00:08:36,140
You would see the log.

176
00:08:36,340 --> 00:08:37,340
The sequence of events.

177
00:08:37,480 --> 00:08:37,600
Right.

178
00:08:37,700 --> 00:08:44,020
It would say door opens, drunk uncle enters, uncle trips on rug, table wobbles, lamp falls.

179
00:08:44,020 --> 00:08:51,360
That is a brilliant way to visualize it, because if you want to fix the bug in a state-based system, you just pick up the lamp and fix the state.

180
00:08:51,920 --> 00:08:53,260
But the uncle is still drunk.

181
00:08:53,540 --> 00:08:53,940
Exactly.

182
00:08:54,160 --> 00:08:55,820
The bug will absolutely happen again.

183
00:08:55,980 --> 00:09:02,480
You have to fix the uncle, but you don't even know the uncle was there because his presence was erased by the state projection.

184
00:09:02,700 --> 00:09:06,680
And this perfectly explains why debugging software is universally miserable.

185
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:07,540
It really is.

186
00:09:07,660 --> 00:09:11,120
The source points out that conventional debuggers are just desperate instruments.

187
00:09:11,120 --> 00:09:17,940
They are tools trying to guess the complex video footage of history from a single degenerate photograph.

188
00:09:18,260 --> 00:09:21,260
After the information has already been permanently destroyed by the CPU.

189
00:09:21,540 --> 00:09:21,800
Wow.

190
00:09:21,960 --> 00:09:25,120
And this really raises the practical cost of this illusion.

191
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:37,160
The source argues that devastating real-world bugs exist entirely because systems conflate distinct histories into the same degenerate state.

192
00:09:37,480 --> 00:09:38,480
Like race conditions, right?

193
00:09:38,480 --> 00:09:38,780
Yes.

194
00:09:39,020 --> 00:09:40,260
Take a race condition, for example.

195
00:09:40,260 --> 00:09:44,080
That's where two parts of a program are trying to do something at the exact same time.

196
00:09:44,200 --> 00:09:44,280
Yeah.

197
00:09:44,380 --> 00:09:47,220
Imagine a multi-threaded ticket purchasing system.

198
00:09:47,340 --> 00:09:47,640
Okay.

199
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:55,460
Thread A is processing your purchase for the last concert ticket, and thread B is processing someone else's purchase for that exact same ticket.

200
00:09:55,620 --> 00:09:56,340
Oh, boy.

201
00:09:56,680 --> 00:10:01,300
The system state tracker just looks at the variable tickets available equals one.

202
00:10:01,300 --> 00:10:06,580
So, both threads check the state, they both see a one, and assume they can proceed.

203
00:10:06,820 --> 00:10:07,160
Correct.

204
00:10:07,160 --> 00:10:10,420
The state projection is non-injective on interleaved histories.

205
00:10:10,720 --> 00:10:10,940
Meaning?

206
00:10:11,240 --> 00:10:17,800
The history where thread A checks first and the history where thread B checks first, they both look identical to the state tracker at that split second.

207
00:10:17,860 --> 00:10:18,500
Oh, man.

208
00:10:18,500 --> 00:10:24,160
So, both threads sell the ticket, the inventory drops to negative one, and the system catastrophically crashes.

209
00:10:24,400 --> 00:10:25,420
Just a total nightmare.

210
00:10:25,420 --> 00:10:33,420
And the same goes for use after free vulnerabilities in memory management, which, you know, are responsible for countless security breaches.

211
00:10:33,480 --> 00:10:33,960
Oh, yeah.

212
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:35,320
Those are huge.

213
00:10:35,560 --> 00:10:40,400
They arise because the state conflates histories that differ only in deallocation events.

214
00:10:40,960 --> 00:10:44,260
The system simply forgets the history of what happened to the memory pointer.

215
00:10:44,260 --> 00:10:54,400
Okay, so if discarding history is the root of our computational problems, the obvious next step is to ask, well, what happens if we build a language where history is the only thing that's real?

216
00:10:54,520 --> 00:10:54,780
Right.

217
00:10:54,940 --> 00:10:57,740
What if we don't even have variables in the traditional sense?

218
00:10:57,860 --> 00:11:00,080
That brings us to the SpherePop inversion.

219
00:11:00,300 --> 00:11:01,520
The SpherePop inversion.

220
00:11:01,620 --> 00:11:05,540
Yeah, SpherePop proposes the exact opposite ontological priority.

221
00:11:06,120 --> 00:11:11,380
Instead of history being an afterthought that you definitely try to reconstruct during debugging.

222
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:13,320
Right, trying to piece together the photograph.

223
00:11:13,320 --> 00:11:13,960
Exactly.

224
00:11:14,300 --> 00:11:15,300
History comes first.

225
00:11:15,600 --> 00:11:19,780
State is entirely derived, secondary, and strictly temporary.

226
00:11:20,020 --> 00:11:21,620
It completely flips the script.

227
00:11:21,820 --> 00:11:28,500
Instead of a program being a sequence of state transformations, the system runs on what the text calls a world structure.

228
00:11:28,940 --> 00:11:32,460
And this world structure consists of just two things.

229
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:38,420
First, a history, which is defined as an ever-growing finite sequence of events.

230
00:11:38,580 --> 00:11:38,800
Right.

231
00:11:38,800 --> 00:11:46,020
And second, an option space, which is the finite set of available symbols or possibilities currently open to the program.

232
00:11:46,220 --> 00:11:47,500
Notice what is missing there.

233
00:11:47,680 --> 00:11:48,020
State.

234
00:11:48,460 --> 00:11:51,280
State is totally absent from the foundational structure.

235
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:51,860
Exactly.

236
00:11:51,860 --> 00:11:58,920
Instead, the computation is driven by four primitive operators that interact strictly with the option space and the history.

237
00:11:59,080 --> 00:12:03,660
Okay, I want to build a mini-program live right now to understand how these four primitives actually work.

238
00:12:03,740 --> 00:12:04,240
Let's do it.

239
00:12:04,240 --> 00:12:08,440
Because they are so counterintuitive if you are used to normal coding.

240
00:12:08,700 --> 00:12:12,720
Let's imagine we are writing a sphere pop program to make a cup of coffee.

241
00:12:12,940 --> 00:12:13,560
Okay, I like it.

242
00:12:13,780 --> 00:12:15,120
The first primitive is pop.

243
00:12:15,460 --> 00:12:16,220
P-O-P, pop.

244
00:12:16,360 --> 00:12:16,540
Right.

245
00:12:16,880 --> 00:12:21,240
The text says pop commits to a symbol, essentially removing it from the option space.

246
00:12:21,420 --> 00:12:23,440
So what does that mean for our coffee maker?

247
00:12:23,440 --> 00:12:27,680
Your initial option space for the coffee maker includes everything it could possibly do.

248
00:12:27,840 --> 00:12:27,980
Right.

249
00:12:28,260 --> 00:12:31,960
Make espresso, make a latte, dispense hot water, turn off.

250
00:12:32,040 --> 00:12:33,080
Okay, all the possibilities.

251
00:12:33,580 --> 00:12:37,680
When the program executes a pop on espresso, it is an act of consumption.

252
00:12:38,280 --> 00:12:42,240
You are taking the latte and hot water possibilities off the table entirely.

253
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:43,080
Oh, I see.

254
00:12:43,460 --> 00:12:48,560
The option space shrinks and the event, Joe's espresso, is appended to the history.

255
00:12:48,880 --> 00:12:50,940
Okay, so pop is a permanent commitment.

256
00:12:51,240 --> 00:12:51,440
Yes.

257
00:12:51,440 --> 00:12:53,240
The second primitive is refuse.

258
00:12:53,980 --> 00:12:57,100
Now the text says refuse documents that a path is inadmissible,

259
00:12:57,320 --> 00:13:01,680
but it does this without removing the structural option from the option space.

260
00:13:01,860 --> 00:13:02,080
Right.

261
00:13:02,320 --> 00:13:03,840
This sounds like a contradiction to me.

262
00:13:03,920 --> 00:13:06,260
I mean, if I refuse something, isn't it just gone?

263
00:13:06,640 --> 00:13:08,040
This is a crucial distinction.

264
00:13:08,340 --> 00:13:09,800
Let's say your next step is to add milk.

265
00:13:09,940 --> 00:13:12,860
So you attempt to pop the add milk option.

266
00:13:12,980 --> 00:13:13,300
Okay.

267
00:13:13,600 --> 00:13:15,760
But the sensor detects the milk reservoir is empty.

268
00:13:16,360 --> 00:13:17,560
The system executes a refuse.

269
00:13:17,560 --> 00:13:21,700
It doesn't say the concept of adding milk no longer exists in this universe.

270
00:13:22,100 --> 00:13:23,760
Because the machine still has a milk button.

271
00:13:23,860 --> 00:13:24,260
Exactly.

272
00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:29,540
It says, I have evaluated the add milk option right now, given the current historical context,

273
00:13:29,740 --> 00:13:32,900
and documented a specific reason why we cannot take it.

274
00:13:33,020 --> 00:13:33,460
Wow.

275
00:13:33,660 --> 00:13:35,720
It records inadmissibility into the history.

276
00:13:35,720 --> 00:13:42,200
So the history log now says chose espresso, refused to add milk due to empty reservoir.

277
00:13:42,380 --> 00:13:42,780
Exactly.

278
00:13:43,320 --> 00:13:47,700
And then third is bind, which simply records a dependency between two things in the history.

279
00:13:47,900 --> 00:13:52,080
So like binding the temperature of the water to the type of roast you popped earlier?

280
00:13:52,340 --> 00:13:53,120
Spot on.

281
00:13:53,200 --> 00:13:58,680
And fourth is collapse, which makes an observation of the history, which we will get to later.

282
00:13:58,680 --> 00:14:02,660
Because none of these operators are mutating a value in a memory slot.

283
00:14:02,860 --> 00:14:03,380
None of them.

284
00:14:03,440 --> 00:14:08,360
They are just appending events to the history log and shrinking the space of what is actually possible next.

285
00:14:08,540 --> 00:14:12,760
And this leads directly to one of the most beautiful mathematical properties in the text.

286
00:14:13,420 --> 00:14:14,900
Theorem 2.7.

287
00:14:15,060 --> 00:14:15,340
Yes.

288
00:14:15,560 --> 00:14:17,220
The conservation of possibility law.

289
00:14:17,300 --> 00:14:19,260
The conservation of possibility law.

290
00:14:19,340 --> 00:14:24,900
It is a conservation law, much like the conservation of mass or energy in physics, but applied to computer science.

291
00:14:25,060 --> 00:14:25,360
Okay.

292
00:14:25,480 --> 00:14:26,020
Break that down.

293
00:14:26,020 --> 00:14:35,840
Under pure POP dynamics, the law states that the size of the history, plus the size of the current option space, will always equal the size of the initial option space.

294
00:14:36,240 --> 00:14:36,760
Oh, wow.

295
00:14:36,980 --> 00:14:45,720
So using the mathematical notation from the source, the absolute value of h sub t plus the absolute value of omega sub t equals omega sub zero.

296
00:14:45,900 --> 00:14:46,340
Exactly.

297
00:14:46,560 --> 00:14:50,540
So as the history log gets longer, the option space gets proportionally smaller.

298
00:14:51,260 --> 00:14:53,900
Possibility cannot be created or destroyed in this system.

299
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:54,260
Never.

300
00:14:54,260 --> 00:14:58,000
It is only consumed by a POP or documented by a refuse.

301
00:14:58,240 --> 00:15:01,640
The total computational material is completely invariant.

302
00:15:01,740 --> 00:15:02,080
That's right.

303
00:15:02,160 --> 00:15:04,680
The total information in the system is totally constant.

304
00:15:04,940 --> 00:15:07,380
It makes me think of carving a block of marble.

305
00:15:07,680 --> 00:15:08,640
Oh, that's a good analogy.

306
00:15:08,780 --> 00:15:11,400
The uncarked block is your initial option space.

307
00:15:11,580 --> 00:15:15,120
I mean, it could be a statue of a horse, a person, a bull.

308
00:15:15,460 --> 00:15:16,840
It could literally be anything.

309
00:15:17,240 --> 00:15:17,460
Right.

310
00:15:17,460 --> 00:15:19,880
Every strike of your chisel is a POP.

311
00:15:20,460 --> 00:15:24,260
It permanently removes a piece of marble, an option, from the block.

312
00:15:24,360 --> 00:15:24,560
Yeah.

313
00:15:24,840 --> 00:15:27,660
You can never sculpt that specific piece of marble again.

314
00:15:27,840 --> 00:15:33,740
But simultaneously, that strike adds a permanent feature to the history, which is the actual sculpture taking shape.

315
00:15:33,920 --> 00:15:36,340
And just like carving stone, you can never put the marble back.

316
00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:36,840
Right.

317
00:15:36,840 --> 00:15:40,240
The history is strictly monotonically increasing.

318
00:15:40,900 --> 00:15:42,280
It is completely irreversible.

319
00:15:42,840 --> 00:15:45,280
And this isn't just theoretical poetry, you know.

320
00:15:45,480 --> 00:15:45,760
Right.

321
00:15:45,980 --> 00:15:50,340
The developers strictly adhered to this philosophy when actually writing the code base.

322
00:15:50,500 --> 00:15:55,520
The text includes snippets from the actual Rust implementation of SpherePop.

323
00:15:55,520 --> 00:15:56,960
Yeah, I saw those.

324
00:15:57,080 --> 00:16:03,260
If you look at the file core slash world dotters, the world struct is defined with exactly two fields.

325
00:16:04,300 --> 00:16:08,380
Pub options of type option space and pub history of type history.

326
00:16:08,500 --> 00:16:11,100
There is literally no state field.

327
00:16:11,280 --> 00:16:11,480
None.

328
00:16:11,700 --> 00:16:13,100
They refuse to build one.

329
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:16,940
The absence of a state field is the mechanization of theorem 2.7.

330
00:16:17,680 --> 00:16:20,700
The philosophy literally forced the code's architecture.

331
00:16:20,860 --> 00:16:21,720
That's wild.

332
00:16:21,720 --> 00:16:27,060
They refuse to store observable state permanently because, ontologically, it isn't real.

333
00:16:27,260 --> 00:16:30,480
Okay, I have to stop you here, though, and put on my practical programmer hat.

334
00:16:30,640 --> 00:16:31,040
Okay.

335
00:16:31,180 --> 00:16:34,700
Because if I am listening to this, I am screaming about memory usage right now.

336
00:16:34,780 --> 00:16:35,120
Oh, sure.

337
00:16:35,240 --> 00:16:42,700
If every single thing I do just appends to an ever-growing history log and we never overwrite state, my laptop is going to literally burst into flames.

338
00:16:42,820 --> 00:16:42,980
Right.

339
00:16:43,080 --> 00:16:43,960
That's the immediate fear.

340
00:16:43,960 --> 00:16:51,320
I mean, if a loop runs a million times, I have a million events in memory instead of just one integer that updated a million times.

341
00:16:52,060 --> 00:16:55,360
How does SpherePop avoid instantly crashing the RAM?

342
00:16:55,780 --> 00:16:58,180
It's the most common and intuitive objection.

343
00:16:58,940 --> 00:17:02,300
If history constantly grows, surely we run out of space.

344
00:17:02,640 --> 00:17:05,920
But you have to remember the other side of theorem 2.7.

345
00:17:06,020 --> 00:17:06,960
The conservation of possibility.

346
00:17:07,720 --> 00:17:08,240
Exactly.

347
00:17:08,540 --> 00:17:11,260
As history grows, the option space shrinks.

348
00:17:11,260 --> 00:17:14,500
But practically, how does that actually balance out in hardware?

349
00:17:14,920 --> 00:17:18,440
Through aggressive use of persistent data structures and structural sharing.

350
00:17:18,640 --> 00:17:19,620
Structural sharing.

351
00:17:19,740 --> 00:17:19,880
Yeah.

352
00:17:20,160 --> 00:17:23,820
When an option is popped, the system doesn't duplicate the entire world.

353
00:17:24,100 --> 00:17:27,080
It just creates a new pointer that shares the previous context.

354
00:17:27,180 --> 00:17:27,680
Oh, I see.

355
00:17:27,900 --> 00:17:33,560
Furthermore, the option space itself is garbage collected as branches of possibility are definitively closed off.

356
00:17:33,760 --> 00:17:35,980
You aren't storing infinite parallel universes.

357
00:17:36,260 --> 00:17:36,520
Right.

358
00:17:36,560 --> 00:17:40,580
You're storing a highly compressed, single-path traversal through a finite graph.

359
00:17:41,260 --> 00:17:46,260
The space required to store the history is offset by the memory freed from the collapsed option space.

360
00:17:46,700 --> 00:17:48,480
So the complexity is conserved.

361
00:17:48,580 --> 00:17:49,000
Precisely.

362
00:17:49,100 --> 00:17:50,180
That makes a lot of sense.

363
00:17:50,280 --> 00:17:53,660
It's not like copying the whole block of marble every time I strike the chisel.

364
00:17:53,820 --> 00:17:56,140
It's just recording the coordinates of the strike.

365
00:17:56,240 --> 00:17:56,440
Right.

366
00:17:56,580 --> 00:17:56,980
Exactly.

367
00:17:56,980 --> 00:18:04,540
But, okay, if we are navigating this ever-trinking block of marble, permanently removing options and committing to paths,

368
00:18:05,160 --> 00:18:08,000
how do I know I haven't carved myself into a corner?

369
00:18:08,260 --> 00:18:08,780
What do you mean?

370
00:18:08,780 --> 00:18:14,980
If there is no state to check, how does the system know my next chisel strike isn't just a fatal error?

371
00:18:15,540 --> 00:18:22,160
Like, in C or Java, the compiler yells at me if I try to put a string of text into a bucket meant for an integer.

372
00:18:22,360 --> 00:18:22,540
Right.

373
00:18:22,760 --> 00:18:23,280
Type checking.

374
00:18:23,700 --> 00:18:23,940
Yeah.

375
00:18:24,120 --> 00:18:26,060
If there are no buckets here, what protects me?

376
00:18:26,060 --> 00:18:29,820
That is where the concept of types completely changes in SpherePop.

377
00:18:30,020 --> 00:18:30,240
Okay.

378
00:18:31,020 --> 00:18:34,360
In conventional languages, a type is, as you said, exactly like a bucket.

379
00:18:34,680 --> 00:18:38,760
If a variable is an integer type, it just means it belongs to the set of whole numbers.

380
00:18:38,900 --> 00:18:39,100
Right.

381
00:18:39,180 --> 00:18:40,980
The compiler just checks the shape of the bucket.

382
00:18:41,180 --> 00:18:43,740
It asks, does this data fit in the integer bucket?

383
00:18:44,080 --> 00:18:47,400
But in SpherePop, types do not describe sets of values.

384
00:18:48,040 --> 00:18:48,320
Wait.

385
00:18:48,600 --> 00:18:48,900
Really?

386
00:18:49,120 --> 00:18:49,380
Really.

387
00:18:49,780 --> 00:18:51,240
Types are reachability certificates.

388
00:18:51,400 --> 00:18:52,260
Reachability certificates.

389
00:18:52,260 --> 00:18:52,940
Yes.

390
00:18:53,840 --> 00:18:57,540
They are mathematical proofs of the history of how a term was produced.

391
00:18:58,540 --> 00:19:05,200
A value of a certain type is the result of a path through history that reached that value without violating any constraints.

392
00:19:05,360 --> 00:19:06,080
Oh, okay.

393
00:19:06,200 --> 00:19:10,500
So it's less like a bucket checking a shape and more like a passport filled with stamps.

394
00:19:10,680 --> 00:19:10,900
Yes.

395
00:19:11,040 --> 00:19:16,420
The passport proves everywhere you traveled, every border you crossed, to get to your current destination legally.

396
00:19:16,740 --> 00:19:17,200
Exactly.

397
00:19:17,720 --> 00:19:19,260
The type certifies the journey.

398
00:19:19,260 --> 00:19:21,960
Let's break down the specific types the system uses.

399
00:19:22,260 --> 00:19:28,120
We have admissible T, which is a certificate proving that the historical path that produced this term was completely valid.

400
00:19:28,340 --> 00:19:28,580
Okay.

401
00:19:28,860 --> 00:19:33,480
Then we have collapsed T, comma, C, which seals an observation under a specific rule.

402
00:19:33,620 --> 00:19:33,880
Right.

403
00:19:34,220 --> 00:19:38,200
But the most interesting and powerful one is refused T, comma, R.

404
00:19:38,440 --> 00:19:41,360
Oh, the refuse primitive we talked about earlier with the coffee maker.

405
00:19:41,420 --> 00:19:41,760
Yes.

406
00:19:42,200 --> 00:19:45,980
Crucially, the refused type inherently carries the reason for refusal.

407
00:19:46,180 --> 00:19:46,640
Oh.

408
00:19:46,640 --> 00:19:49,120
Denoted by that, R in the type signature.

409
00:19:49,260 --> 00:19:53,540
If you try to do something illegal, like adding milk when the reservoir is empty.

410
00:19:53,660 --> 00:19:55,660
The system doesn't just throw an exception or crash.

411
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:57,960
Or return a generic null error.

412
00:19:58,120 --> 00:19:58,220
Right.

413
00:19:58,300 --> 00:19:58,460
Yeah.

414
00:19:58,580 --> 00:20:03,960
It generates a type that encapsulates the exact historical context of why the path was blocked.

415
00:20:04,320 --> 00:20:11,320
So a constraint violation is fundamentally a different mathematical type than a generic not available error.

416
00:20:11,600 --> 00:20:12,020
Correct.

417
00:20:12,020 --> 00:20:18,080
Two terms refused for different reasons have genuinely different mathematical types.

418
00:20:18,180 --> 00:20:18,540
Wow.

419
00:20:18,540 --> 00:20:21,680
Because the reason is permanently embedded as part of the type.

420
00:20:22,120 --> 00:20:25,160
This creates a system where refusal isn't a failure.

421
00:20:25,480 --> 00:20:29,040
It is documented inadmissibility with a permanent certificate.

422
00:20:29,420 --> 00:20:29,880
That's wild.

423
00:20:29,880 --> 00:20:32,160
You can pass that refusal around in your code.

424
00:20:32,280 --> 00:20:32,440
Yeah.

425
00:20:32,660 --> 00:20:38,140
Analyze it and branch logic off of it because the refusal itself is a first class citizen of the history.

426
00:20:38,540 --> 00:20:39,560
That is incredibly rigorous.

427
00:20:39,820 --> 00:20:40,200
It is.

428
00:20:40,460 --> 00:20:45,440
But, I mean, implementing something that strict led to a massive internal conflict for the developers, didn't it?

429
00:20:45,440 --> 00:20:50,920
The text dedicates a significant portion to this wild clash they ran into regarding the open and close world.

430
00:20:51,040 --> 00:20:51,220
Yes.

431
00:20:51,320 --> 00:21:00,540
This is one of the most fascinating anecdotes in the whole development process because it shows how abstract philosophy suddenly becomes a very real engineering roadblock.

432
00:21:00,660 --> 00:21:00,760
Right.

433
00:21:00,820 --> 00:21:06,660
When the developers were building the interpreter for SpherePop, they encountered a major problem with free variables.

434
00:21:07,320 --> 00:21:10,240
Free variables meaning variables that are undeclared or unknown.

435
00:21:10,580 --> 00:21:14,980
Like if I just write I plus 5 but I never told the computer what Y actually is.

436
00:21:14,980 --> 00:21:15,500
Precisely.

437
00:21:16,280 --> 00:21:22,180
Now the interpreter, which executes the code, treated an unknown variable as a symbolic atom.

438
00:21:22,380 --> 00:21:22,700
Meaning?

439
00:21:22,840 --> 00:21:24,340
Just an unresolved possibility.

440
00:21:24,480 --> 00:21:29,880
It essentially said, I don't know what Y is yet, but I will keep it in the option space just in case.

441
00:21:29,980 --> 00:21:30,240
Okay.

442
00:21:30,360 --> 00:21:31,220
That seems reasonable.

443
00:21:31,480 --> 00:21:37,300
But the type checker, which is designed to prove validity, immediately rejected Y as a hard error.

444
00:21:37,380 --> 00:21:37,780
Oh.

445
00:21:38,200 --> 00:21:40,980
It said, this isn't declared, therefore it is illegal.

446
00:21:41,540 --> 00:21:41,780
Wait.

447
00:21:41,780 --> 00:21:42,860
I have to push back here.

448
00:21:43,200 --> 00:21:44,540
Isn't the type checker just right?

449
00:21:44,980 --> 00:21:45,180
No.

450
00:21:45,240 --> 00:21:49,040
I mean, if a variable isn't defined in the code, isn't that just a typo?

451
00:21:49,500 --> 00:21:49,980
A bug?

452
00:21:50,060 --> 00:21:51,660
Like I just forgot to declare why.

453
00:21:51,820 --> 00:21:56,920
Why is this a profound philosophical discovery and not just a standard syntax error that they needed to patch?

454
00:21:57,080 --> 00:22:00,860
Because it forces a decision about what an unknown actually represents in reality.

455
00:22:01,160 --> 00:22:04,280
Does the absence of a declaration mean the object doesn't exist?

456
00:22:04,780 --> 00:22:07,440
Or does it just mean we haven't decided what it is yet?

457
00:22:07,680 --> 00:22:08,160
Oh.

458
00:22:08,160 --> 00:22:14,200
The developers realized they weren't just fighting over a bug, they were clashing over epistemology.

459
00:22:14,360 --> 00:22:16,320
The philosophical theory of knowledge.

460
00:22:16,500 --> 00:22:16,840
Exactly.

461
00:22:16,980 --> 00:22:17,280
I see.

462
00:22:17,920 --> 00:22:21,440
But how does a type checker mechanize epistemology?

463
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:25,680
It depends entirely on the boundary of the world you assume you are operating in.

464
00:22:25,720 --> 00:22:26,000
Right.

465
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:31,420
A closed world context assumes we have complete total knowledge of the system.

466
00:22:32,040 --> 00:22:39,120
In a closed world, if something is unknown, it means it does not exist and therefore trying to use it is an error.

467
00:22:39,320 --> 00:22:42,060
Which is how almost all traditional compilers work.

468
00:22:42,180 --> 00:22:43,780
They assume they can see the whole board.

469
00:22:43,960 --> 00:22:44,040
Yeah.

470
00:22:44,120 --> 00:22:45,940
If it's not in the dictionary, it's just not a word.

471
00:22:46,080 --> 00:22:46,420
Exactly.

472
00:22:46,860 --> 00:22:49,260
But the alternative is an open world context.

473
00:22:49,360 --> 00:22:49,600
Okay.

474
00:22:49,760 --> 00:22:51,540
This assumes we only have partial knowledge.

475
00:22:51,980 --> 00:22:54,760
We are operating in a universe larger than our current perception.

476
00:22:54,920 --> 00:22:55,120
Right.

477
00:22:55,320 --> 00:22:58,600
In an open world, an unknown variable is not an error.

478
00:22:59,080 --> 00:23:01,280
It is simply undecided possibility.

479
00:23:01,520 --> 00:23:01,840
Oh, what?

480
00:23:01,840 --> 00:23:04,260
It exists as an unresolved manifold.

481
00:23:05,200 --> 00:23:11,280
The type checker assigns it the type unit, meaning this is an unconstrained path that we might travel down later.

482
00:23:11,280 --> 00:23:14,420
This makes me think of, like, a robot navigating a room.

483
00:23:14,600 --> 00:23:14,860
Sure.

484
00:23:15,140 --> 00:23:23,580
If the robot's camera doesn't see a chair in the corner, a closed world AI assumes the corner is empty and might just try to drive straight through it.

485
00:23:23,660 --> 00:23:23,900
Yes.

486
00:23:24,180 --> 00:23:26,940
But an open world AI assumes the corner is unknown.

487
00:23:27,580 --> 00:23:30,580
Like, there might be a chair there, it just hasn't observed it yet.

488
00:23:30,600 --> 00:23:31,700
That's a perfect analogy.

489
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:36,140
So both the interpreter and the type checker in SpherePop were mathematically correct.

490
00:23:36,140 --> 00:23:41,840
They were just operating under entirely different philosophical assumptions about the boundaries of reality.

491
00:23:42,160 --> 00:23:42,380
Yes.

492
00:23:42,680 --> 00:23:47,620
And the developers realized they couldn't just fix the bug by making one side win.

493
00:23:48,080 --> 00:23:48,420
Right.

494
00:23:48,540 --> 00:23:49,700
Because both are valid.

495
00:23:49,760 --> 00:23:54,400
Because depending on what you are building, you might need a closed world or you might need an open world.

496
00:23:54,560 --> 00:23:54,760
Okay.

497
00:23:54,820 --> 00:23:55,600
So what did they do?

498
00:23:56,060 --> 00:24:00,160
They had to introduce a new construct called type mode into the rest code.

499
00:24:00,580 --> 00:24:00,700
Yeah.

500
00:24:00,880 --> 00:24:06,000
Type mode is an explicit declaration of whether the compiler should operate in a closed world or an open world state.

501
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:09,980
They literally had to code their philosophical stance into an enum.

502
00:24:10,240 --> 00:24:10,880
They had to.

503
00:24:11,060 --> 00:24:11,260
Yeah.

504
00:24:11,860 --> 00:24:21,460
A language whose fundamental ontology is about navigating a possibility space must explicitly state its epistemological boundaries.

505
00:24:21,560 --> 00:24:21,940
Right.

506
00:24:21,940 --> 00:24:28,180
It forces the programmer to declare, do I know everything about this domain or am I leaving room for the unknown?

507
00:24:28,420 --> 00:24:29,360
I love that.

508
00:24:29,740 --> 00:24:30,700
I absolutely love that.

509
00:24:30,740 --> 00:24:31,260
It's pretty amazing.

510
00:24:31,620 --> 00:24:31,800
Okay.

511
00:24:31,800 --> 00:24:41,680
So we have an irreversible history of events and we have types that act as passports proving our legal journey through possibility space and we've established the boundaries of our world.

512
00:24:42,460 --> 00:24:43,640
But let's be real for a second.

513
00:24:44,320 --> 00:24:48,380
Eventually, a human being needs to look at the computer screen and actually see an output.

514
00:24:48,620 --> 00:24:48,920
Of course.

515
00:24:48,920 --> 00:24:51,100
I need to see if my bank balance is $500.

516
00:24:51,540 --> 00:24:52,500
I need to see a state.

517
00:24:52,760 --> 00:24:52,940
Yes.

518
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:57,980
So how do we look at history without ruining the philosophy and just falling back into the state illusion?

519
00:24:58,440 --> 00:25:00,580
This is where we reach the geometry of observation.

520
00:25:01,680 --> 00:25:08,120
The source text reveals that observable state is not a real physical thing stored in the computer's memory.

521
00:25:08,300 --> 00:25:08,560
Okay.

522
00:25:08,760 --> 00:25:10,080
It is a quotient of history.

523
00:25:10,260 --> 00:25:11,540
A quotient like in division.

524
00:25:11,680 --> 00:25:13,500
Can you explain how that applies here?

525
00:25:13,500 --> 00:25:21,940
In mathematics, a quotient space is formed by taking a larger set and grouping items into equivalence classes based on some specific rule.

526
00:25:22,060 --> 00:25:22,340
Okay.

527
00:25:22,640 --> 00:25:23,760
You are folding the space.

528
00:25:24,400 --> 00:25:29,160
In sphere pop, the state you observe depends entirely on how you look at the history.

529
00:25:29,340 --> 00:25:29,620
Right.

530
00:25:29,860 --> 00:25:34,340
And how you look at it requires what the text calls a collapse rule.

531
00:25:34,580 --> 00:25:35,020
Collapse.

532
00:25:35,200 --> 00:25:39,780
You apply a collapse rule to the history and it generates the state for you at that exact moment.

533
00:25:39,780 --> 00:25:40,220
Okay.

534
00:25:40,360 --> 00:25:43,340
Let's walk through the four canonical rules of collapse mentioned in the text.

535
00:25:43,500 --> 00:25:46,580
Because they perfectly illustrate how a state is just a projection.

536
00:25:46,860 --> 00:25:47,280
Let's do it.

537
00:25:47,380 --> 00:25:48,740
The first is the identity rule.

538
00:25:49,020 --> 00:25:49,500
Identity.

539
00:25:49,560 --> 00:25:51,320
This is the finest, most granular rule.

540
00:25:51,800 --> 00:25:54,760
Under identity, every single event in the history is visible.

541
00:25:54,920 --> 00:25:56,260
So no compression whatsoever.

542
00:25:56,380 --> 00:25:56,660
Exactly.

543
00:25:56,820 --> 00:26:00,300
The state you see is exactly equal to the raw history log.

544
00:26:00,620 --> 00:26:00,880
Okay.

545
00:26:01,060 --> 00:26:02,880
The second is the last right rule.

546
00:26:03,180 --> 00:26:08,080
And this seems like it's essentially the conventional view of state in modern programming.

547
00:26:08,080 --> 00:26:08,640
Yes.

548
00:26:09,120 --> 00:26:14,560
Last right maps the history by only caring about the most recent assignment to a variable.

549
00:26:14,760 --> 00:26:15,460
Oh, right.

550
00:26:15,560 --> 00:26:22,140
If your history shows you assigned x to 1, then x to 2, then x to 3, applying the last

551
00:26:22,140 --> 00:26:25,860
right rule collapses all of that and only shows you that x is 3.

552
00:26:26,220 --> 00:26:29,300
It retroactively ignores all the previous pops.

553
00:26:29,480 --> 00:26:29,720
Yes.

554
00:26:29,860 --> 00:26:32,020
It creates the state illusion on demand.

555
00:26:32,260 --> 00:26:32,640
Wow.

556
00:26:32,640 --> 00:26:34,780
The third is the accumulate rule.

557
00:26:35,260 --> 00:26:39,600
This one counts the events, but it entirely forgets the order in which they happened.

558
00:26:40,020 --> 00:26:40,980
It's order forgetful.

559
00:26:41,040 --> 00:26:41,220
Right.

560
00:26:41,400 --> 00:26:44,880
Like knowing I drank three cups of coffee, but not knowing if I drank them in the morning

561
00:26:44,880 --> 00:26:45,460
or at night.

562
00:26:45,640 --> 00:26:46,400
Perfect example.

563
00:26:46,760 --> 00:26:48,120
And finally, the projection rule.

564
00:26:48,800 --> 00:26:54,320
This rule filters the history events by a specific label prefix, only showing you a

565
00:26:54,320 --> 00:26:56,780
slice of the history that matches your specific criteria.

566
00:26:57,320 --> 00:26:58,660
So ignoring everything else.

567
00:26:58,780 --> 00:26:59,000
Right.

568
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:02,160
Thinking about these collapse rules, a visual metaphor comes to mind.

569
00:27:02,160 --> 00:27:02,700
Let's hear it.

570
00:27:02,740 --> 00:27:05,440
It's like looking at a three-dimensional cylinder floating in a room.

571
00:27:05,780 --> 00:27:10,320
If your rule is look at the cylinder from directly above, it collapses into a two-dimensional

572
00:27:10,320 --> 00:27:10,740
circle.

573
00:27:10,900 --> 00:27:11,120
Yes.

574
00:27:11,240 --> 00:27:15,060
If your rule is look at it from the side, it collapses into a two-dimensional rectangle.

575
00:27:15,240 --> 00:27:15,560
Exactly.

576
00:27:15,780 --> 00:27:18,540
A circle and a rectangle look like completely different shapes.

577
00:27:18,620 --> 00:27:21,820
I mean, if you showed them to two different people, they would argue about what object they

578
00:27:21,820 --> 00:27:22,300
were looking at.

579
00:27:22,300 --> 00:27:23,340
They absolutely would.

580
00:27:23,680 --> 00:27:26,960
But the cylinder itself, the history, it never changed.

581
00:27:27,380 --> 00:27:29,780
Only the observer's angle, the rule, did.

582
00:27:29,780 --> 00:27:31,920
That is a perfect topological analogy.

583
00:27:32,660 --> 00:27:38,680
The history is the 3D object, and the state is the 2D shadow it casts, depending on where

584
00:27:38,680 --> 00:27:39,520
you place the light.

585
00:27:39,640 --> 00:27:40,600
Oh, I like that.

586
00:27:40,940 --> 00:27:44,400
And this aligns directly with the CLIO connection mentioned in the text.

587
00:27:44,820 --> 00:27:48,420
CLIO stands for constraint-limited information ontology, right?

588
00:27:48,600 --> 00:27:48,900
Yes.

589
00:27:49,080 --> 00:27:50,560
How does that apply to our cylinder?

590
00:27:50,560 --> 00:27:56,640
CLIO is a framework that models how representations restructure semantic space.

591
00:27:58,080 --> 00:28:05,040
CLIO argues that representations, or in our case, collapse rules, don't just compress data.

592
00:28:05,260 --> 00:28:08,500
They fundamentally alter the topology of what is distinguishable.

593
00:28:08,820 --> 00:28:09,180
Exactly.

594
00:28:09,380 --> 00:28:11,280
They change what shadows can be cast.

595
00:28:11,620 --> 00:28:15,380
For example, under the accumulate rule, two completely different histories.

596
00:28:15,380 --> 00:28:19,420
Like doing task A, then task B, versus doing task B, then task A.

597
00:28:19,480 --> 00:28:19,580
Right.

598
00:28:19,760 --> 00:28:22,540
Those cast the exact same shadow under accumulate.

599
00:28:23,180 --> 00:28:26,660
Because the event counts are the same, they look identical.

600
00:28:26,920 --> 00:28:28,420
They are topologically identified.

601
00:28:28,720 --> 00:28:33,280
But under the identity rule, they cast different shadows because the order actually matters.

602
00:28:33,800 --> 00:28:36,860
So state is entirely relative to the observer's rule.

603
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:38,900
There is no such thing as the true state.

604
00:28:39,140 --> 00:28:39,340
No.

605
00:28:39,460 --> 00:28:42,000
There is only the state given rule X.

606
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:42,480
Yes.

607
00:28:42,900 --> 00:28:45,440
And the SpherePop implementation reflects this beautifully.

608
00:28:45,900 --> 00:28:51,420
In the observable state module, state is derived on demand using a function called derive,

609
00:28:51,880 --> 00:28:54,020
which takes history and rule as arguments.

610
00:28:54,140 --> 00:28:54,620
Oh, wow.

611
00:28:54,700 --> 00:28:56,700
Observable state is never stored permanently.

612
00:28:56,880 --> 00:28:57,620
It's just a shadow.

613
00:28:57,980 --> 00:28:58,160
Right.

614
00:28:58,240 --> 00:29:03,100
It is a fleeting projection, a shadow, calculated at the exact moment a human needs to look at

615
00:29:03,100 --> 00:29:04,920
it, and then it is immediately discarded.

616
00:29:05,140 --> 00:29:05,360
Okay.

617
00:29:05,460 --> 00:29:10,440
So if state is just a fleeting shadow and types are just travel logs, what happens to the most

618
00:29:10,440 --> 00:29:12,560
sacred cow of computer science?

619
00:29:12,680 --> 00:29:13,040
Which is-

620
00:29:13,040 --> 00:29:16,940
The function- I mean, functional programming is practically a religion for a lot of developers.

621
00:29:17,180 --> 00:29:17,740
It really is.

622
00:29:17,840 --> 00:29:19,280
Everything is built on functions.

623
00:29:19,940 --> 00:29:21,540
How does SpherePop handle them?

624
00:29:21,820 --> 00:29:24,740
SpherePop radically reverses the priority of functions.

625
00:29:24,880 --> 00:29:25,240
How so?

626
00:29:25,640 --> 00:29:29,180
In standard computing, functions are the base reality.

627
00:29:29,780 --> 00:29:32,460
A program is just applying functions to values.

628
00:29:32,620 --> 00:29:32,920
Right.

629
00:29:32,920 --> 00:29:35,400
But in SpherePop, functions are not primitive.

630
00:29:35,920 --> 00:29:39,520
They are just a convenient notation for a specific historical pattern.

631
00:29:39,520 --> 00:29:43,640
The text defines a function as deferred commitment.

632
00:29:44,120 --> 00:29:44,860
Deferred commitment.

633
00:29:45,080 --> 00:29:46,300
I want to unpack that carefully.

634
00:29:46,460 --> 00:29:46,580
Yeah.

635
00:29:46,760 --> 00:29:50,740
When I write a function in a normal language, say a simple function that takes a variable

636
00:29:50,740 --> 00:29:55,820
y, multiplies it by two, and returns the result, what is that in SpherePop?

637
00:29:56,140 --> 00:30:00,400
When you define that function, you are creating what SpherePop calls a closure.

638
00:30:00,720 --> 00:30:00,980
Right.

639
00:30:01,500 --> 00:30:03,280
But the text redefines a closure.

640
00:30:03,540 --> 00:30:05,940
It's not a machine waiting for inputs.

641
00:30:06,120 --> 00:30:07,720
It is a frozen possibility.

642
00:30:07,840 --> 00:30:08,620
Frozen possibility.

643
00:30:08,620 --> 00:30:14,360
Is a partial, unrealized future suspended along with its historical context, just waiting

644
00:30:14,360 --> 00:30:15,960
for a pop event to actualize it.

645
00:30:15,960 --> 00:30:20,820
You are saying, I have this timeline mapped out where something gets multiplied by two,

646
00:30:21,300 --> 00:30:24,620
but I am deferring the commitment of what that something actually is.

647
00:30:24,720 --> 00:30:28,320
And when I finally call that function in my code and pass it the number five...

648
00:30:28,320 --> 00:30:33,340
That process, formerly known as beta reduction in lambda calculus, is actually just a special

649
00:30:33,340 --> 00:30:34,200
case of a collapse.

650
00:30:34,720 --> 00:30:35,440
Wait, really?

651
00:30:35,440 --> 00:30:35,920
Yeah.

652
00:30:35,920 --> 00:30:39,400
When you apply the argument five, you are generating a pop event.

653
00:30:40,100 --> 00:30:44,920
You are committing the variable y to the value five, which removes all other possibilities

654
00:30:44,920 --> 00:30:49,640
for i from the option space, thaws the frozen timeline, and resumes the history.

655
00:30:49,640 --> 00:30:53,920
So a function isn't a factory that takes raw materials and spits out a product.

656
00:30:54,120 --> 00:30:54,200
No.

657
00:30:54,300 --> 00:30:59,040
It's a paused timeline waiting for a specific historical event to kickstart it again.

658
00:30:59,120 --> 00:30:59,500
Exactly.

659
00:30:59,660 --> 00:31:01,360
That is a massive paradigm shift.

660
00:31:01,680 --> 00:31:06,120
And reading through the paper, it becomes clear this has riled implications for how you

661
00:31:06,120 --> 00:31:07,860
actually build a compiler for this language.

662
00:31:07,920 --> 00:31:10,220
Oh, it breaks traditional compiler correctness completely.

663
00:31:10,220 --> 00:31:12,900
Let's look at how conventional compilers are tested.

664
00:31:13,300 --> 00:31:17,040
A compiler translates human-readable code into machine code.

665
00:31:17,620 --> 00:31:22,600
In traditional language design, a compiler is considered correct if the compiled machine

666
00:31:22,600 --> 00:31:26,520
code produces the exact same output value as the interpreted code.

667
00:31:26,640 --> 00:31:26,920
Right.

668
00:31:27,120 --> 00:31:32,120
If they both output the number 42, the compiler is certified as correct.

669
00:31:32,560 --> 00:31:35,900
But SpherePop says that is totally insufficient.

670
00:31:36,280 --> 00:31:36,660
Completely.

671
00:31:36,660 --> 00:31:42,200
Which makes sense because, as we established, the final output, the 42, is just a degenerate

672
00:31:42,200 --> 00:31:43,000
state projection.

673
00:31:43,260 --> 00:31:46,000
It's just the index card that says the butler is in jail.

674
00:31:46,180 --> 00:31:46,660
Exactly.

675
00:31:47,340 --> 00:31:52,200
Two programs that produce the same final value of 42 may have constructed radically different

676
00:31:52,200 --> 00:31:53,280
histories to get there.

677
00:31:53,440 --> 00:31:55,220
One might have added 20 and 22.

678
00:31:55,780 --> 00:31:57,940
Another might have multiplied 21 by 2.

679
00:31:58,120 --> 00:32:01,700
Another might have crashed three times, recovered, and then output 42.

680
00:32:02,040 --> 00:32:02,320
Right.

681
00:32:02,520 --> 00:32:05,140
And in SpherePop, the history is the program.

682
00:32:05,140 --> 00:32:07,900
So verifying just the output is completely meaningless.

683
00:32:08,160 --> 00:32:09,560
But hold on, I have to challenge this.

684
00:32:09,640 --> 00:32:10,040
Go for it.

685
00:32:10,260 --> 00:32:12,620
If output equivalence isn't enough, what is?

686
00:32:12,780 --> 00:32:16,000
Does the compiler have to perfectly mimic every single microscopic step?

687
00:32:16,280 --> 00:32:16,580
Yes.

688
00:32:16,760 --> 00:32:16,900
What?

689
00:32:17,200 --> 00:32:19,620
The standards SpherePop uses is history equivalence.

690
00:32:19,880 --> 00:32:25,400
A compiler is only correct if the history constructed by the virtual machine executing the compiled

691
00:32:25,400 --> 00:32:29,940
code matches the history constructed by the interpreter exactly, event for event.

692
00:32:29,940 --> 00:32:30,560
Wow.

693
00:32:31,080 --> 00:32:37,640
The mathematical standard in the paper is I of P dot history equals V of C of P dot history.

694
00:32:38,000 --> 00:32:39,900
Interpreter history equals VM history.

695
00:32:40,120 --> 00:32:40,400
Yes.

696
00:32:40,580 --> 00:32:42,760
So the compiler can't take any clever shortcuts.

697
00:32:42,900 --> 00:32:43,120
Yes.

698
00:32:43,280 --> 00:32:46,920
Because modern compilers like LLVM are famous for optimizing QuarNode.

699
00:32:47,100 --> 00:32:47,340
Right.

700
00:32:47,500 --> 00:32:51,920
If I write a loop that does nothing but count to 10, the compiler might just replace the whole

701
00:32:51,920 --> 00:32:53,780
loop with the number 10 to save time.

702
00:32:53,780 --> 00:32:58,140
But in SpherePop, it can't do that because that alters the sequence of events.

703
00:32:58,320 --> 00:32:58,860
Oh, man.

704
00:32:59,220 --> 00:32:59,460
Correct.

705
00:32:59,920 --> 00:33:02,300
The optimization cannot destroy the history.

706
00:33:03,020 --> 00:33:07,680
And this requirement fundamentally changes what the intermediate representation or IR of

707
00:33:07,680 --> 00:33:09,080
the compiler actually looks like.

708
00:33:09,300 --> 00:33:09,400
Right.

709
00:33:09,720 --> 00:33:14,020
Traditional IR emits instructions to mutate the machine's state in the most efficient way

710
00:33:14,020 --> 00:33:14,440
possible.

711
00:33:14,900 --> 00:33:16,620
It issues commands to overwrite memory.

712
00:33:16,800 --> 00:33:18,460
But SpherePop's IR doesn't do that.

713
00:33:18,720 --> 00:33:21,620
No, it doesn't emit mutating instructions at all.

714
00:33:21,620 --> 00:33:24,980
It emits events to reconstruct history.

715
00:33:25,820 --> 00:33:30,920
The compiler acts as a history reconstruction engine, not a state mutation engine.

716
00:33:31,640 --> 00:33:34,700
This all sounds theoretically perfect and rigorously beautiful.

717
00:33:34,920 --> 00:33:35,640
It really is.

718
00:33:35,940 --> 00:33:40,500
But when this monograph was peer-reviewed, a reviewer raised a massive red flag.

719
00:33:41,260 --> 00:33:45,180
Chapter 18 of the Saurus is entirely dedicated to answering this challenge.

720
00:33:45,380 --> 00:33:45,660
Yes.

721
00:33:45,880 --> 00:33:47,860
The reviewer pointed out a perceived paradox.

722
00:33:47,860 --> 00:33:52,560
They basically said, look, because human programmers eventually have to look at a collapsed

723
00:33:52,560 --> 00:33:57,240
value on a screen, an output shadow, aren't they still suffering from the state illusion?

724
00:33:57,860 --> 00:33:58,680
That's a very fair point.

725
00:33:58,820 --> 00:33:59,040
Right.

726
00:33:59,160 --> 00:33:59,400
Right.

727
00:33:59,560 --> 00:34:03,840
Has SpherePop actually solved anything or did you just relocate the problem to the monitor?

728
00:34:04,060 --> 00:34:05,240
It's a brilliant critique.

729
00:34:05,600 --> 00:34:09,920
The reviewer is essentially saying that as long as a human is in the loop, you just can't

730
00:34:09,920 --> 00:34:10,600
escape state.

731
00:34:10,600 --> 00:34:13,620
And the authors of the monograph address it with brutal honesty.

732
00:34:13,820 --> 00:34:16,860
They do, by introducing the no-direct-observation theorem.

733
00:34:16,980 --> 00:34:19,760
The mathematically rigorous limit of the entire system.

734
00:34:19,800 --> 00:34:20,040
Yes.

735
00:34:20,340 --> 00:34:24,180
The theorem proves that the reviewer is technically correct about the illusion.

736
00:34:24,300 --> 00:34:24,840
Oh, wow.

737
00:34:25,060 --> 00:34:28,040
Because the space of all possible histories is infinite.

738
00:34:28,720 --> 00:34:34,440
And any observable space, like a computer monitor or terminal window or a human retina,

739
00:34:34,680 --> 00:34:35,400
is finite.

740
00:34:35,680 --> 00:34:37,340
So you have the pigeonhole problem again?

741
00:34:37,340 --> 00:34:37,940
Exactly.

742
00:34:38,300 --> 00:34:45,380
Any observation map must be non-injective, meaning some level of state illusion is mathematically

743
00:34:45,380 --> 00:34:46,820
unavoidable in this universe.

744
00:34:46,940 --> 00:34:52,000
No computational system can eliminate the illusion entirely if it wants to actually show a result

745
00:34:52,000 --> 00:34:53,480
to a finite human observer.

746
00:34:53,720 --> 00:34:54,000
Right.

747
00:34:54,400 --> 00:34:55,620
So wait, the reviewer was right.

748
00:34:55,740 --> 00:34:56,660
SpherePop fails.

749
00:34:56,820 --> 00:34:57,060
No.

750
00:34:57,280 --> 00:35:02,300
We did all this complex history tracking, all this option space math, just to end up trapped

751
00:35:02,300 --> 00:35:03,340
by the illusion anyway.

752
00:35:03,440 --> 00:35:06,720
No, because the reviewer was conflating two entirely different concepts.

753
00:35:06,720 --> 00:35:10,780
There is a crucial distinction between state erasure and state illusion.

754
00:35:10,920 --> 00:35:12,540
Okay, we need to clarify those two terms.

755
00:35:12,640 --> 00:35:13,320
How are they different?

756
00:35:13,500 --> 00:35:15,280
Conventional coding suffers from state erasure.

757
00:35:15,700 --> 00:35:19,860
When X changes from 5 to 6, the history of it being 5 is deleted from the RAM.

758
00:35:20,020 --> 00:35:22,880
The system irrecoverably discards the history.

759
00:35:23,440 --> 00:35:24,820
The universe just forgets.

760
00:35:25,140 --> 00:35:25,540
Exactly.

761
00:35:26,300 --> 00:35:31,580
SpherePop, however, cannot eliminate the illusion of observation because of the physics of screens

762
00:35:31,580 --> 00:35:32,780
and eyeballs we just discussed.

763
00:35:32,880 --> 00:35:33,120
Right.

764
00:35:33,120 --> 00:35:35,440
But it completely eliminates the erasure.

765
00:35:35,900 --> 00:35:42,000
Because the full raw history is always kept safe in the world.history log.

766
00:35:42,100 --> 00:35:42,520
Exactly.

767
00:35:42,720 --> 00:35:44,260
The history is retained structurally.

768
00:35:44,920 --> 00:35:49,640
So while the programmer might be looking at a collapsed, illusory state on their screen,

769
00:35:50,160 --> 00:35:52,040
they're never actually trapped by it.

770
00:35:52,200 --> 00:35:52,480
Okay.

771
00:35:52,480 --> 00:35:57,360
If they suspect the observation is misleading, like if they see the lamp on the floor and

772
00:35:57,360 --> 00:36:02,220
wonder if the drunk uncle was there, they can perform what the text calls disambiguation

773
00:36:02,220 --> 00:36:03,060
by refinement.

774
00:36:03,720 --> 00:36:05,000
Disambiguation by refinement.

775
00:36:05,260 --> 00:36:07,220
Meaning they can just change their collapse rule?

776
00:36:07,460 --> 00:36:07,980
Precisely.

777
00:36:08,540 --> 00:36:13,040
Because the history is intact behind the scenes, they can switch from the last right rule to

778
00:36:13,040 --> 00:36:13,920
the identity rule.

779
00:36:14,100 --> 00:36:15,320
A finer collapse rule.

780
00:36:15,360 --> 00:36:16,580
And recover the hidden truth.

781
00:36:16,740 --> 00:36:19,680
They can look at the 3D cylinder from a completely different angle.

782
00:36:19,680 --> 00:36:23,960
In a conventional system, that recovery is physically impossible because the history

783
00:36:23,960 --> 00:36:24,580
was destroyed.

784
00:36:24,940 --> 00:36:25,200
Right.

785
00:36:25,920 --> 00:36:27,840
Sphere pop doesn't abolish the illusion.

786
00:36:28,240 --> 00:36:32,380
It makes the illusion explicit, parameterized, and fully reversible.

787
00:36:32,680 --> 00:36:34,240
It gives you the power to see through it.

788
00:36:34,400 --> 00:36:34,860
Exactly.

789
00:36:35,080 --> 00:36:35,440
I see.

790
00:36:35,520 --> 00:36:36,840
That is a phenomenal defense.

791
00:36:37,340 --> 00:36:43,280
It completely defangs the reviewer's critique and really turns a perceived weakness into a

792
00:36:43,280 --> 00:36:45,600
demonstration of the system's ultimate resilience.

793
00:36:46,320 --> 00:36:49,360
You can't avoid shadows, but you can always move the light.

794
00:36:49,360 --> 00:36:50,320
I love that.

795
00:36:50,820 --> 00:36:55,960
And this realization leads to the final, perhaps most profound evolution in the text.

796
00:36:56,260 --> 00:36:59,400
The forward shift, which they call the V4 architecture.

797
00:36:59,640 --> 00:36:59,880
Yes.

798
00:37:00,620 --> 00:37:03,640
Up until this point in the text, the framework has been backwards looking.

799
00:37:03,780 --> 00:37:03,940
Right.

800
00:37:04,180 --> 00:37:05,540
History records what happened.

801
00:37:06,120 --> 00:37:07,340
Types certify what happened.

802
00:37:07,480 --> 00:37:08,960
It's all about auditing the past.

803
00:37:09,560 --> 00:37:12,500
But the forward shift flips the causal arrow entirely.

804
00:37:12,500 --> 00:37:16,880
It says that histories are not fundamental just because they are records of the past.

805
00:37:17,220 --> 00:37:20,600
They are fundamental because they are operators that deform the future.

806
00:37:20,820 --> 00:37:22,960
This is encapsulated in the two-projection architecture.

807
00:37:23,480 --> 00:37:26,980
In this final model, a program doesn't just map a history to a state.

808
00:37:27,500 --> 00:37:31,020
First, it maps a history to a future reachability geometry.

809
00:37:31,280 --> 00:37:32,380
An admissibility field.

810
00:37:32,380 --> 00:37:32,900
Exactly.

811
00:37:33,340 --> 00:37:38,640
It maps out a geometric field of what futures are still allowed to exist, given the history

812
00:37:38,640 --> 00:37:39,500
that has already occurred.

813
00:37:40,100 --> 00:37:43,780
Every time you pop an option, the geometry of the future morphs and shrinks.

814
00:37:44,220 --> 00:37:50,000
And then the system projects that future geometry into an observable state for the user to see.

815
00:37:50,260 --> 00:37:50,460
Yes.

816
00:37:50,560 --> 00:37:54,040
So a program is not a list of instructions to be blindly executed.

817
00:37:54,240 --> 00:37:56,120
It is a reachability sculpture.

818
00:37:56,380 --> 00:37:56,800
Exactly.

819
00:37:56,800 --> 00:38:02,640
Every pop, every refuse is a chisel stroke carving away the marble of possibility,

820
00:38:03,360 --> 00:38:06,000
shaping an admissibility field of what can happen next.

821
00:38:06,320 --> 00:38:09,180
The program's true purpose isn't to calculate a number.

822
00:38:09,440 --> 00:38:11,340
It's to sculpt the boundaries of the future.

823
00:38:11,640 --> 00:38:13,920
It is a stunning conceptual leap.

824
00:38:14,080 --> 00:38:14,780
It really is.

825
00:38:14,820 --> 00:38:18,620
You stop thinking about what the machine is currently holding in memory, the fleeting state,

826
00:38:18,660 --> 00:38:21,760
and you start thinking entirely about the geometry of accessible futures.

827
00:38:21,960 --> 00:38:24,820
Bringing this massive philosophical journey to a close,

828
00:38:24,820 --> 00:38:27,780
the core takeaway for you, the learner, is really profound.

829
00:38:28,120 --> 00:38:32,620
Sphere pop forces us to fundamentally change our relationship with digital reality.

830
00:38:32,800 --> 00:38:33,020
It does.

831
00:38:33,220 --> 00:38:37,060
We have to stop asking the conventional question, what is the current state?

832
00:38:37,600 --> 00:38:40,220
And we have to start asking two much deeper questions.

833
00:38:40,800 --> 00:38:42,720
What futures have we foreclosed?

834
00:38:42,800 --> 00:38:45,220
And what histories did we construct to get here?

835
00:38:45,500 --> 00:38:50,700
Because computation is an irreversible journey through a geometry of possibilities.

836
00:38:50,700 --> 00:38:54,300
It exposes what computation actually does to possibility space,

837
00:38:54,400 --> 00:38:59,420
rather than summarizing the result as a degenerate projection and just throwing away the evidence.

838
00:38:59,580 --> 00:38:59,680
Right.

839
00:38:59,880 --> 00:39:03,740
And I want to leave you with a final lingering thought that builds on this source material.

840
00:39:04,120 --> 00:39:08,580
Because if computation is just the progressive restriction of possibility...

841
00:39:08,580 --> 00:39:10,100
A series of pops and refusals.

842
00:39:10,180 --> 00:39:10,420
Right.

843
00:39:10,620 --> 00:39:14,100
Shaping an admissibility field of what futures are open to us.

844
00:39:14,500 --> 00:39:17,400
Isn't that exactly how human memory and human life works?

845
00:39:17,480 --> 00:39:18,880
The parallel is striking, honestly.

846
00:39:18,880 --> 00:39:24,240
Every choice you make, every career path you take, every person you decide to marry or not marry,

847
00:39:24,480 --> 00:39:27,000
it permanently forecloses a million other futures.

848
00:39:27,360 --> 00:39:29,840
Your life is an irreversible history log.

849
00:39:29,920 --> 00:39:31,500
You can't go back and unpop a decision.

850
00:39:31,900 --> 00:39:32,300
Exactly.

851
00:39:32,520 --> 00:39:34,880
So the real question is, when you look back in your own life,

852
00:39:35,560 --> 00:39:38,380
what collapse rule are you using to observe your own history?

853
00:39:38,640 --> 00:39:38,960
Wow.

854
00:39:39,000 --> 00:39:43,900
Are you just looking at the last right, focusing only on your current job title or your current bank balance?

855
00:39:43,900 --> 00:39:51,140
Or are you using accumulate, just counting your successes and failures as abstract numbers without any context?

856
00:39:51,460 --> 00:39:54,600
And what hidden truths might you uncover if you change the rule?

857
00:39:54,740 --> 00:39:55,020
Right.

858
00:39:55,420 --> 00:40:02,500
If you applied the identity rule and looked at the full, uncompressed sequence of every tiny event and choice that became who you are...

859
00:40:02,500 --> 00:40:06,700
you might find that the history is far more meaningful than the state you currently occupy.

860
00:40:07,420 --> 00:40:09,760
Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive.

861
00:40:10,140 --> 00:40:13,460
Keep questioning the boundaries of your own worlds, open or closed,

862
00:40:13,580 --> 00:40:15,920
and remember to look at the history, not just the state.

863
00:40:16,520 --> 00:40:17,760
We will see you next time.

